 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to rescinding the anniversary day school holiday for the boroughs of Brooklyn and Queens; and to repeal section 2586 of the education law relating thereto",
    "basePrintNo": "S5217",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5217-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Rpld §2586, amd §3635, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5217\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to rescinding the\nanniversary day school holiday for the boroughs of Brooklyn and\nQueens; and to repeal section 2586 of the education law relating\nthereto\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo rescind the anniversary day school holiday for the boroughs of\nBrooklyn and Queens.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1. Section 2586 of the education law is repealed.\n\nSection 2. Subdivision 2-a of section 3635 of the education law, as\namended by chapter 424 of the laws of 2005, is amended.\n\nSection 3. Contains the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe first celebration of Anniversary Day (as it was then called)\noccurred on May 28, 1861, when the 32nd Anniversary of the Sunday\nSchool Union was commemorated. When Brooklyn and Queens joined New\nYork City in 1898, there was pressure to include Queens. In 1905, the\nLegislature enacted a law making the first Thursday in June an annual\nholiday for Brooklyn's public school children to celebrate \"the\nfounding of the Sunday School movement in Brooklyn.\" Fifty years\nlater, as more of the population moved to Queens, the name was changed\nto Brooklyn-Queens Day.\n\nThe original emphasis has shifted and the religious origins of\nBrooklyn-Queens Day have been ignored. It has become a secular holiday\nfor which schools still remain closed.\n\nThere is no reason to continue this anachronistic holiday in State\nstatutes when there is pressure to increase the time students spend in\nschool. For this reason, schools should not be closed.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nPreviously introduced.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nFirst of January next succeeding the date on which it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
